{
  "concept": "Gaseous diffusion is the gradual mixing of gases driven by random molecular motion down a concentration gradient, and lighter gas molecules (H2) spread faster than heavier ones (O2).",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "two round glass bulbs (flasks) with pale blue glass outlines, connected by a narrow horizontal tube",
    "stopcock valve drawn in the connecting tube, shown closed (blocking) at time 0 and open thereafter",
    "small white dots representing H2 molecules, initially all in the left bulb, labeled 'H2'",
    "small red dots representing O2 molecules, initially densely packed in the right bulb, labeled 'O2'",
    "panel-stage captions: 'Stopcock closed (a)', 'Stopcock open (b)', 'Some time after Stopcock open (c)' shown as the elapsed-time indicator",
    "color-by-mass legend appearing when the mass-coding toggle is active"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "elapsedTime",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Elapsed time since stopcock opened",
      "range": [0, 100, 1],
      "default": 0,
      "teaches": "Diffusion is a continuous, gradual process — at 0 the gases are fully separated, around the middle H2 has visibly crossed faster than O2, and at the end both gases are uniformly mixed; intermediate times reveal there is no sharp boundary, just steady equilibration"
    },
    {
      "id": "diffusionAnimation",
      "type": "animation",
      "label": "Play diffusion",
      "loop": false,
      "teaches": "Watching the stopcock open and the particles drift in real time shows that mixing emerges from many random molecular motions, not from any directed force pulling the gases together"
    },
    {
      "id": "massColoring",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Emphasize molecular mass",
      "options": ["Standard colors", "Color by mass"],
      "default": "Standard colors",
      "teaches": "Color-coding by mass (light H2 vs heavy O2) makes it visible that the lighter gas empties its bulb and invades the other side sooner — the microscopic basis for Graham's law, where rate scales inversely with the square root of molar mass"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Stopcock closed",
      "narration": "With the stopcock closed, all white H2 molecules are trapped in the left bulb and all red O2 molecules fill the right bulb — two pure gases, no exchange possible.",
      "state": { "elapsedTime": 0, "massColoring": "Standard colors" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Stopcock just opened",
      "narration": "Shortly after opening, some H2 has already spread into the right bulb while only a few heavier O2 molecules have crossed left — the lighter gas diffuses noticeably faster.",
      "state": { "elapsedTime": 45, "massColoring": "Standard colors" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Compare by mass",
      "narration": "Switch to color-by-mass coding at this intermediate moment: the imbalance becomes unmistakable — the light-mass population has penetrated much farther than the heavy-mass one.",
      "state": { "elapsedTime": 45, "massColoring": "Color by mass" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Equilibrium",
      "narration": "Given enough time, random motion erases the concentration gradient entirely: both gases are distributed uniformly across both bulbs, and net movement stops even though molecules never stop moving.",
      "state": { "elapsedTime": 100, "massColoring": "Standard colors" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Diffusion needs no driving force beyond random molecular motion, and lighter molecules diffuse faster — which is why H2 crosses into the O2 bulb before O2 meaningfully crosses back, foreshadowing Graham's law.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Render one apparatus (two side-by-side round bulbs joined by a short tube with a stopcock in the middle) rather than three separate panels; the slider/animation reproduces panels (a) → (b) → (c) as states of the same drawing. Keep roughly 30–40 white H2 dots and 25–30 red O2 dots, with the red dots drawn slightly larger to suggest greater mass. At time 0 the stopcock valve handle is horizontal/closed and the tube is visually blocked; once time advances, the handle rotates to open. Distribution at intermediate time should clearly favor H2 crossing (roughly a third of H2 in the right bulb, only a few O2 in the left); at full time, both colors are near-uniformly mixed across both bulbs. Caption beneath the apparatus updates with slider position: 'Stopcock closed (a)' → 'Stopcock open (b)' → 'Some time after stopcock open (c)'. In 'Color by mass' mode, tint particles on a light-to-heavy scale (e.g., pale cyan for H2, deep maroon for O2) with a small legend showing the mass gradient. Pale blue-gray glass, white background, dots small and unstroked."
}

